Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery Unit Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  BARBARA LYNN SMITH  M D  Signed Out by  Melinda J Lerwill  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Addendum  2  Additional Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization  FISH  Results CLINICAL DATA  New calcifications  prior L cancer  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  INVASIVE AND IN SITU DUCTAL CARCINOMA WITH CALCIFICATIONS  SEE NOTE AND TABLE  1  NOTE  The invasive carcinoma has both conventional and papillary morphologies  The papillary regions demonstrate microscopically irregular contours and a complex infolding growth pattern  consistent with invasion  Immunostains for p63  calponin  SM MHC  and cytokeratin 14 fail to demonstrate myoepithelial cells around these foci  consistent with a diagnosis of invasive carcinoma when considered with the morphology  Other areas with more rounded  circumscribed nests of ductal carcinoma also lack myoepithelial cells by immunohistochemical evaluation  and these may represent ductal carcinoma in situ with marked myoepithelial cell attenuation or possibly additional bluntly invasive carcinoma  The current carcinoma demonstrates morphologic similarity to the patient s prior carcinoma   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Multiple levels examined  TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS  1 INVASIVE CARCINOMA TUMOR SIZE  At least 0 2cm  GRADE  1  LYMPHATIC VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  BLOOD VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  MARGIN OF INVASIVE CARCINOMA  Invasive carcinoma comes to  0 1cm from the posterior margin and to 0 12cm from the inferior margin  and a focus suspicious for invasion comes to  0 1cm from the medial margin  LOCATION OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Within and beyond the region of the invasive carcinoma  GRADE OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  1  MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Ductal carcinoma in situ comes to  0 1cm from the medial  inferior  and posterior margins  That some of these foci interpreted as in situ carcinoma may actually represent bluntly invasive carcinoma cannot be entirely excluded  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN  and  left breast biopsy with needle loc   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm  tan yellow to focally hemorrhagic portion of irregular fibroadipose tissue with a short suture designating superior  a long suture designating lateral  and a needle localization wire in place  The specimen is inked according to protocol  Anterior   blue  posterior   black  superior   green  inferior   yellow  and serially sectioned from medial to lateral  Sectioning reveals a lobulated  tan yellow cut surface with focal hemorrhagic areas  No discrete masses or lesions are grossly identified  The specimen is submitted entirely as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial en face margin   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Body of specimen   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral en face margin  The area of possible calcifications is in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Final Diagnosis by Melinda J Lerwill M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1  QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assesse